import{_ as t,o,c as a,a3 as n}from"./chunks/framework.BwzYn71E.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"1.1 So Tell Me: What Is Production Controlled by Blocking?","description":"","frontmatter":{"lang":"en-US","translationSource":{"title":"1\\\\.1 那我问你，什么是阻塞生产","archivedAt":"2026-10-23T14:20:34.857Z","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=quUTb9JSv879txqKEKI0BS","scope":"page"}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"src/page-quUTb9JSv879txqKEKI0BS.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-quUTb9JSv879txqKEKI0BS.md","lastUpdated":null}'),i={name:"src/page-quUTb9JSv879txqKEKI0BS.md"};function s(r,e,l,c,p,u){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<div class="logistics-content"><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="_1-1-那我问你-什么是阻塞生产" aria-hidden="true"></span><h1 id="_1-1-so-tell-me-what-is-production-controlled-by-blocking" tabindex="-1">1.1 So Tell Me: What Is Production Controlled by Blocking? <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-1-so-tell-me-what-is-production-controlled-by-blocking" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1\\.1 So Tell Me: What Is Production Controlled by Blocking?&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>quUTb9JSv879txqKEKI0BS</code> | <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=quUTb9JSv879txqKEKI0BS" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p>In automatic industrial production in Endfield, intelligent components—such as a valve opening when inventory reaches a threshold—are lacking. At present, perhaps the only widely used signals are <span class="mark-red"><strong>clock signals and blocking signals</strong></span>. This tutorial mainly discusses <span class="mark-red"><strong>blocking signals</strong></span> in automatic production.</p><blockquote><p>Clock signals can also produce remarkable lines. For example, I used them for single-Forge Heavy Xiranite production <a href="./page-zfM2Zf9DLRvNytZWjXcqpG">Single-Forge Heavy Xiranite Using Clock Signals</a>. The signal-control discussion in the lower half of this page also covers this. Other members are welcome to cite related results here.</p><p><a href="./page-sZuRWF2FUyP9OGGU2XJnPP">Producing Xircon Effluent in a Single Reactor Crucible</a></p></blockquote><p>Just as a flowing-to-blocked transition in a belt/splitter/converger system causes <u>flow redistribution</u> (see <a href="./page-hDaaudSDbsi3GGvVV2XWBL">Flow Theory for Idealized Logistics Systems</a>, <a href="./page-SI1crfpBOI1mEcT4TsqTBX">4.0 Building the Science of Blocking-Based Production on Blocking-Flow Theory (Slow Updates)</a>), <strong>blocking during production redistributes flow and thereby changes production behavior</strong>. We can use this for <strong>automatic production switching</strong>, serving more advanced objectives.</p><blockquote><p>Automatic switching means that, without manual player intervention such as rearranging or changing facility states, a line changes its production scheme using internally generated signals such as blocking or periodicity.</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Such production is, to an extent, equivalent to mathematically solving flows on a blocking graph. Compared with blocking-flow theory, however, it adds machines that transform item streams, connecting more closely to practical applications while introducing many new problems.</p></blockquote><p>Several practically meaningful signals can be reduced to blocking signals in particular circumstances:</p><ol><li><p><span class="mark-purple">A machine produces at full or a specified rate → its input buffer blocks, except at the critical case where supply rate equals production rate.</span></p></li><li><p><span class="mark-purple">Product storage is full → the producing machine&#39;s output buffer blocks, and its input buffer blocks as a consequence.</span></p></li><li><p><span class="mark-purple">Flow reaches 6/m → a control port limited to 6 creates blocking.</span></p></li><li><p><span class="mark-purple">In fluid-gas-transmuter feedback, Liquid Xiranite is supplied only when the input fluid is sufficient → the input fluid blocks the Liquid Xiranite recirculation loop, routing it into the transmuter&#39;s supply path.</span></p></li></ol><p>We can therefore deliberately build potentially blocking structures around production needs and use the flow changes between blocking states for automatic production. The principles address <strong>how to construct these structures, under what conditions they block, how that blocking redistributes production-line flows, and whether the final objective is met</strong>.</p><p>This is no mysterious advanced theory. Think one step further about your existing lines; you may already have encountered or used it in many places. Recognizing the phenomenon is more important.</p><blockquote><p>Reaching storage capacity is one form of blocking, so how lines change when storage fills is an important topic too (full-storage-dynamics.jpg).</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>If basic line design means making products at rate y* from a given quantity or input rate x*—the finished report looks correct—f(x*) = y*, this article discusses the next level: how the line responds to external changes such as input supply or full product storage, and how to use those responses for more complex production needs. Does f give the correct y for every x in some range? When supply x* changes, does y* reflect the desired result? Or when y* changes, can x* respond appropriately?</p></blockquote><p><a href="./page-vjw47WlrDGyaYlemKyfnH8">1.2 What Are the Benefits of Blocking-Based Production?</a></p></div>',1)])])}const g=t(i,[["render",s]]);export{h as __pageData,g as default};
